- Set the due day when the question can be resolved. It falls after the item's day, and on or after the day the material that settles the question is published.
- For a figure, use its publication date, which falls after the end of the period the figure covers: stated when the topic or the item gives it, expected_publication when you set the day you expect it to be published. Company figures use the company's fiscal year: "this year" contains the item's publication day, and "next year" follows it; convert fiscal years using the fiscal-year note. Other figures use calendar years.
- For an event, name the date at which its outcome is assessed: stated when the topic or the item gives it; period_end when it is the last day of the period the topic names, or the company's current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision that has no date.
- When the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation, the due day is at most 548 days (18 months) after the item: for an obligation already taken on, its next annual filing; for one not yet signed, the day by which the signing can be checked.
Before you return the answer, check it once. Read the question back: does it ask one thing, the one the topic's outcome names, and is every other part of the topic's description and outcome that the question leaves out listed in not_judged? For a figure, check that each range starts exactly where the one below it ends. For an event, check that happened is the event the question asks about; that ruled_out names who decides and a decision of theirs against that event as a whole or its abandonment, never the due day arriving or the event merely being still awaited, and is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out; and that each can be checked on the due day. Check that a reference's kind says truly whether its words state an expectation or the amount already reported for the same quantity, that its value is the number its words state, and, for a figure, that the value falls in the middle range, or that the middle range runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, that every bound is a figure and not a future target, and that the due day follows the rules above. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, check that favourable_end is lower unless the topic or the item settles the other end or the topic's own words say that more of it is good. Correct the answer where a check fails.{
